        "source_text": "\n      Definition and fact — $\\tau$-nets, covering numbers, a Lipschitz margin\n      \n        A finite $Z\\subseteq X$ is a $\\tau$-net if every $x\\in X$ has some $z\\in Z$ with $\\|x-z\\|\\le\\tau$; the covering number $M(\\tau,X)$ is the smallest size of one. Cubes of side $2\\tau/\\sqrt d$ have half-diagonal $\\tau$, so $M(\\tau,[0,1]^d)\\le\\lceil\\sqrt d/(2\\tau)\\rceil^d$: for $\\tau=0.1$, $64$ points in $d=2$ but $16^{10}\\approx1.1\\cdot10^{12}$ in $d=10$. Fact: if $h$ is $L$-Lipschitz, $Z$ is a $\\tau$-net of $X$ and $h(z)\\ge L\\tau$ on $Z$, then $h\\ge0$ on $X$, because $h(x)\\ge h(z)-L\\|x-z\\|\\ge L\\tau-L\\tau=0$. $\\blacksquare$\n      \n    ",
        "text_sha256": "616ed69b1acfde49afdbe60cf295bb0f204aed8e0728bc1288b0248b7e51b96c",
        "source_sha256": "a5f4bb40de256c39cd355e7f47f73375ba9244875b6bf5482f8dee849e6ad780",
        "inventory_kind": "source_unit_needs_claim_review"
      },
